## 20. Governance checklist

### Govern

- Name the business owner.
- Name the technical owner.
- Define approved use cases.
- Define restricted data fields.
- Define expert-routing approval rules.
- Define escalation queues.

### Map

- Map products, components, specialties, experts, queues, and support levels.
- Map which topics can be answered from world knowledge.
- Map which topics require internal knowledge.
- Map which topics require human escalation.

### Measure

- Track routing accuracy.
- Track no-match rate.
- Track expert override rate.
- Track unsafe-output blocks.
- Track PII redactions.
- Track hallucination guardrail triggers.
- Track average clarification count.

### Manage

- Review failed routes.
- Refresh stale expert data.
- Add missing product-topic mappings.
- Tune matching weights.
- Update guardrail thresholds.
- Add eval cases for new failures.
- Review security and privacy incidents.

---

## 21. Deployment checklist

Before deployment, confirm:

- Expert database fields are classified as user-visible, internal-only, or never-expose.
- Database queries are parameterized and field-limited.
- Input guardrails are active.
- Tool guardrails are active before and after expert database calls.
- Output guardrails are active.
- Handoff summary generation masks unnecessary PII.
- Human approval exists for sensitive write actions.
- The agent can say `I don't know` when no source is available.
- The agent has tests for jailbreak, prompt injection, PII leakage, hallucination, stale data, and ambiguous routing.
- Routing decisions are logged without sensitive data.

